In article <1991Jun18.191119.14044@jpl-devvax.jpl.nasa.gov>, cah@jpl-devvax.jpl.nasa.gov (Allen Halsell) writes:

> I have read some of the recent messages about color in the
> Nethack/UNIX world.  I'm running with terminfo set, and it's true, I
> get a little salmon-colored rectangle for a player.  Is this it?  I'm
> sure it's not, but I don't know where to go from here.

> I'm running on a SPARC IPC under Motif.  Can someone please help me
> out?  BTW, I'm sort of a UNIX plebe, but I can get around OK.  

If you are using the color xterm that I hacked, or some other terminal
program that uses the standard ANSI colors, you'll have to change the
color definitions in termcap.c.  I use these:

#define COLOR_BLACK   7		/* fake out to avoid black on black */
#define COLOR_BLUE    4
#define COLOR_GREEN   2
#define COLOR_CYAN    6
#define COLOR_RED     1
#define COLOR_MAGENTA 5
#define COLOR_YELLOW  3
#define COLOR_WHITE   7


In any case, you should be getting an @ for the player no matter what
you're using.

Oh, and here's my tic destcription for the terminal:

vt100-c|Vt 100 terminal with ANSI color support,
	colors#8, setf=\E[3%p1%dm, setb=\E[4%p1%dm, bold=\E[21m, use=vt100,

If these don't work, a more complete description of what you've done
already and what your environment is might help.

